{%- comment -%}
  Resolves a colour option value to a hex and prints the chip.

  This was previously done with a CSS attribute map keyed off
  data-colour="{{ value | handleize }}". That failed silently: an option value
  is a drop rather than a string, so handleize returned nothing, every selector
  missed, and the swatches rendered empty. Resolving in Liquid and writing the
  colour inline removes the whole class of failure — nothing depends on an
  attribute matching a stylesheet.

  Order of preference:
    1. a swatch the merchant set on the option value in admin
    2. the SIDIZ palette below, matched on the colour name
    3. a neutral placeholder, so a chip never renders invisibly

  Accepts: value (option value), selected (boolean)
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  capture, not a filter chain. An option value is a drop: it prints its name
  through {{ }}, but passing it straight into strip or downcase yields an empty
  string, so every lookup missed and every chip fell through to the placeholder.
  Capturing forces the drop to render to text first.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture value_text %}{{ value }}{% endcapture -%}

{%- liquid
  assign name = value_text | strip
  assign key = name | downcase | replace: ' ', '' | replace: '-', ''

  assign hex = ''
  if value.swatch.color
    assign hex = value.swatch.color
  endif

  if hex == ''
    case key
      when 'shadowgrey'
        assign hex = '#4a4744'
      when 'tanbrown'
        assign hex = '#835740'
      when 'creambeige'
        assign hex = '#d4d1c3'
      when 'greenblack'
        assign hex = '#222728'
      when 'grey', 'gray'
        assign hex = '#989b98'
      when 'black'
        assign hex = '#2c2d2f'
      when 'dryrose'
        assign hex = '#946661'
      when 'daisyyellow'
        assign hex = '#ba8b42'
      when 'greyishmint', 'grayishmint'
        assign hex = '#87999b'
      when 'tealblue'
        assign hex = '#4e686d'
      when 'deepnavy'
        assign hex = '#2f3842'
      when 'ivory'
        assign hex = '#f0eadf'
      when 'lightgrey', 'lightgray'
        assign hex = '#c4c8cb'
      when 'darkgrey', 'darkgray'
        assign hex = '#5a5d60'
      when 'warmgrey', 'warmgray'
        assign hex = '#a39c95'
      when 'deepgreen'
        assign hex = '#38463c'
      when 'terracotta'
        assign hex = '#b56a4d'
      when 'khaki'
        assign hex = '#7c7455'
      when 'warmblue'
        assign hex = '#6b8199'
      when 'ashgreen'
        assign hex = '#9aa694'
      when 'rosiepink'
        assign hex = '#e0a9ae'
      when 'lavenderblue'
        assign hex = '#b3bcd8'
      when 'soymilkbeige'
        assign hex = '#e8ddc9'
      when 'white'
        assign hex = '#f4f4f4'
      else
        assign hex = '#d6dade'
    endcase
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  Drawn as an SVG rather than a coloured div.

  The div version kept rendering as an empty ring: its size comes from a
  four-level descendant chain in the KR sheet, and wherever that chain does not
  match the box collapses and the colour has nothing to paint. An SVG carries
  its own intrinsic size and paints via fill, so no stylesheet can flatten it.
{%- endcomment -%}
<span class="color-chip" data-colour="{{ key }}" title="{{ name | escape }}">
  <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 28 28" role="img" aria-label="{{ name | escape }}" focusable="false">
    <circle cx="14" cy="14" r="10" fill="{{ hex }}" stroke="rgba(0,0,0,0.12)" stroke-width="1"/>
  </svg>
</span>
